finding: 1. SkinnedMesh removal leaves an invalid live palette consumer. src/extras/gltf/skinning.ts:251-255 releases the rows, World.remove clears firstJoint to 0, but skinningVertex at :175-190 unconditionally uses that value for a MeshMaterial that still names SkinnedMaterial. A removed entity therefore skins using the unrelated allocation at palette row 0. src/extras/gltf/scene.ts:407,434-439 also continues writing a removed instance's pose into that row without checking membership. Independently reproduced on HEAD with two one-joint instances: A firstJoint=0, B firstJoint=1; remove B's SkinnedMesh, spawn C reusing row 1; B retains MeshMaterial and upload supplies B offset 0. Posing removed B changes A's palette translation from -50 to 0. The lifetime test (skinning.gpu.test.ts:343-364) checks only extent and destroys the removed entity before another draw; it misses the invalid consumer and cross-instance corruption. Removal must have an explicit safe rendering/posing outcome, not alias another entity's allocation. 2. Culling verification cannot distinguish entity-local bounds from world-space or double-scaled bounds. src/extras/gltf/skinning.gpu.test.ts:248 and :366-387 spawn the culling subject at identity placement. Its bind-pose-versus-posed pixel assertion is useful, but world-space bounds and placement applied twice are identical to the correct answer under this placement. The nonidentity-placement owner test checks GlobalTransform and gltf.bounds, not MeshInstance.cullBounds or rendered culling. The current writeSkinPose bounds arithmetic appears entity-local; the claimed regression protection needs a translated/scaled placement where the incorrect-space/scale answer fails. 3. Nonzero-family-base deformation readback covers color only, not prepass/shadows. src/extras/gltf/skinning.gpu.test.ts:139 creates the ReadSkin material as AlphaMode.Blend; its camera has neither DepthPrepass nor lights. Thus :291-330 verifies every vertex and recorded time at the nonzero base only through the color lane. The separate floor/batching subject (:389-429) enables depth and sun shadows but uses one mesh at base zero and only asserts that depth/shadow views exist plus the color draw's indirect count. Wrong vidx/deformation in a depth or shadow path can pass. Add an observable posed result at a nonzero family base in those paths rather than treating pipeline/view creation as deformation parity. Independently read the requested stage, measurement note, specification clauses, CONTRIBUTING, range diff and all seven commit messages. Checks: bun test src/extras/gltf (104/0); animation.parity.node.ts plus material-varyings.node.ts (47/0); render allocation oracle (7/0, all measured windows zero unadmitted bytes). Real Apple M4 Max Metal adapter. Runtime reproduction above made no file changes. No pre-land check or lavapipe run. Sound: 256-texel palette width means 64 joints per row; four column-major texels reconstruct each matrix; u16 packing/unpacking and global times inverse-bind agree with the specification. Readback CPU reference uses recorded worlds and decoded inverse binds, independently of palette staging, and compares every vertex at several recorded times. Both textures are forced through post-warm replacement and high-eid/later-joint-row addressing. Allocation holes coalesce and extent trims, but consumer lifetime remains the finding above. Raw palette textures ARE world-owned: world.gpu.device.createTexture is intercepted by gpu.ts:1486-1490 and calls owner.own; no missing ownership finding. Ignored mesh-node transforms and unskinned single-decomposition bits are covered and the extracted unskinned pose arithmetic is preserved. Varying repair retains IO decoration while stripping value structs; existing smooth and new exact integer observations pass color/prepass/sun/point tests. Pre-boxing caller times does not exclude pose/palette/bounds/upload from allocation sampling. Replacement-material JSDoc names bind-pose fallback, bindings and undeformed tangents. No additional naming finding. Uncertainty: did not independently regenerate the large golden files or repeat browser/lavapipe evidence. The two verification findings identify blind spots, not demonstrated errors in the present bounds math or shared pass implementations.
